WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:02.490
Hey guys, it's Jan here!

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:06.870
I've just seen the new Deadpool movie, and
it's clear that right from the opening credits,

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:11.460
Deadpool knows exactly what kind of movie
it is and makes absolutely no bones about

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.640
it whatsoever.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.131
Yes, this Deadpool is super-meta, super-cheeky,
super-violent and... super-fun.

00:00:19.131 --> 00:00:24.640
Forget Green Lantern Hal Jordan, Wade Wilson
aka Deadpool is the comic-book role that Ryan

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.730
Reynolds was born to play.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:32.190
And I don't mean the Merc with a Sewn-up Mouth
who we saw in X-Men Origins: Wolverine, but

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:39.850
the full-on potty-mouthed Merc with filter-free
Verbal Diarrhoea and a chronic case of ADHD

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:45.559
who Reynolds brings to delirious life here
with bags of obnoxious charm and a sack full

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:46.730
of sass.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.440
From start to finish, Reynolds plays the part
to perfection, fully embracing Deadpool's

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.330
fourth-wall-breaking world of R-rated violence,
nudity, and language.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:59.770
Reynolds completely carries the whole movie
on his shoulders, and if you were ever in

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:04.699
any doubt that he was totally up to that task,
you can't have seen his gripping turn in the

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:09.979
incredibly tense thriller Buried, which featured
Reynolds alone on screen for 90 minutes with

00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:14.250
nothing but a wooden coffin, a lighter, and
a cell phone to keep him company.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:19.719
And just as many of us can't imagine anyone
other than Robert Downey Jr as Iron Man since

00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:25.429
he pulled on that exosuit back in 2008, as
soon as you've seen Ryan Reynolds as Deadpool,

00:01:25.429 --> 00:01:28.509
you won't want anyone else in that red costume.

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:33.429
And, frankly, based on this film, I hope they
make more Deadpool movies with Reynolds in

00:01:33.429 --> 00:01:34.429
the lead.

00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:38.159
As for the movie's supporting characters,
well, Deadpool's deliberately overbearing

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:43.119
personality puts everyone pretty much in the
shade, and they're intentionally full of played-up

00:01:43.119 --> 00:01:44.340
stereotypes.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:50.369
From Ed Skrein's British villain role as Deadpool's
nemesis Ajax to Karan Soni's Indian taxi driver.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:55.799
I enjoyed the back-and-forth between Deadpool
and the totally CG-created Colossus, voiced

00:01:55.799 --> 00:01:57.889
with a Russian accent by Stefan Kapicic.

00:01:57.889 --> 00:02:02.299
And I really loved Brianna Hildebrand as Negasonic
Teenage Warhead.

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:05.359
I'd definitely like to see more of her in
future movies.

00:02:05.359 --> 00:02:10.500
TJ Miller gets some nice interaction with
Deadpool as the Merc with a Mouth's pal Weasel.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.090
While former MMA star Gina Carano gets to
bust balls as Ajax's hyper-strong henchwoman,

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.090
Angel Dust.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:21.689
As Wade Wilson's girlfriend Vanessa, Morena
Baccarin is great – she has good chemistry

00:02:21.689 --> 00:02:24.000
with Reynolds and gives as good as she gets.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.360
I'd like to see her character developed and
the same goes for Deadpool's flatmate Blind

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.920
Al, who's played by Leslie Uggams, though
of course this particular movie is Mr Pool's

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:33.980
story, not theirs.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.500
Director Tim Miller, who's known for his visual
effects, commercials, and video game work,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.819
has done a fantastic job – in fact, it's
incredible to think that this is his first

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:43.819
feature film.

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:50.510
Note to X-Men Origins: Wolverine director
Gavin Hood, this is how you use slow-mo, not

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.200
the overly melodramatic rubbish used in Wolverine's
origin tale!!

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.670
Miller has put together a stylish film and
made it look as if the cast and crew had a

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.090
ball making it.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:06.129
The movie's 108 minutes speed by in a hail
of gunfire, gags, and profanity, thanks to

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:10.569
a script by Zombieland writers Rhett Reese
and Paul Wernick which creatively more-or-less

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:15.680
masks the movie's tight-budget and cleverly
avoids the bit in a superhero origin tale

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.230
where we wait for the hero to become, you
know, super.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:24.030
That the movie's story is pretty low-stakes
doesn't really matter – it's not about what

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:28.640
happens so much as it is about getting to
know and love this incarnation of the stunningly

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.049
self-aware comic-book character at the centre
of it all.

00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:36.639
Humour comes thick and fast and covers all
points from the juvenile to the shrewdly ironic

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:41.129
so expect everything from the scatological
to slapstick, self-referential, and more.

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:45.219
Some of the jokes didn't land for me, but
the sheer volume of them meant that there

00:03:45.219 --> 00:03:48.590
was usually something on the way pretty soon
that did.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:53.499
In terms of other movies, Deadpool's stylised
violence, hyper-genre-awareness, and superhero

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:58.219
shtick compares favourably to the likes of
Kick-Ass, Kingsman: The Secret Service, and

00:03:58.219 --> 00:03:59.670
James Gunn's movie Super.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.370
So, shut up, crime!

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:06.000
The old-school vibe of the movie's soundtrack,
which is a suitably eclectic mix of everything

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.639
from rap to easy listening and 80s pop to
soft rock, reminded me of Guardians of the

00:04:10.639 --> 00:04:16.730
Galaxy, while the movie's original score is
by JunkieXL, who also scored the insanely-inspired

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.549
Mad Max: Fury Road.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:22.530
As you'd imagine, there's a shed-load of Easter
Eggs and references to the comic books, other

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.540
movies, and general pop culture.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.349
In fact, I'll have a lot more discussion about
that in my next video, which I'll add a link

00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:31.550
to here when it's ready for you to watch!

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:37.050
Oh, and expect a pretty cool cameo from Stan
Lee, some very Deadpool animated credits and

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.260
also a little something after the credits.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.100
Oscar-winning Costume Designer Angus Strathie
and Production Designer Sean Haworth, whose

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:48.270
work you'll have seen in the recent Goosebumps
movie, have done some fine work creating Deadpool's

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.270
world.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.210
And Make-up Designer Bill Corso, who transformed
Steve Carell for the movie Foxcatcher, has

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:58.830
done similarly stellar work again in this
movie, especially with Wade Wilson's post-Workshop

00:04:58.830 --> 00:04:59.830
look.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:03.520
I also want to give a shout-out to the movie's
Stunt Co-ordinators Robert Alonzo and Philip

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:07.581
Silvera, who, between them, have worked on
movies including Star Trek, Iron Man, and

00:05:07.581 --> 00:05:09.840
Netflix's Daredevil series.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.570
The fragmented fight scenes and action sequences
in Deadpool are top-notch.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:19.440
So, to sum up: Tim Miller's Deadpool movie
is the best Deadpool movie that chimichanga-loving

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.729
comic book fans could have wished for.

00:05:21.729 --> 00:05:26.169
Its star is pretty much pitch-perfect, its
action and violence are loud and proud, its

00:05:26.169 --> 00:05:31.090
language is totally untamed, and its tongue
is very firmly in its cheek as it takes a

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:36.580
full-frontal swipe at superhero clichés while
merrily making the most of them itself.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.319
Deadpool gets 4 out of 5 Flicks.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:41.289
Now, what are you looking forward to most
about Deadpool?

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:43.760
Or, if you've seen it, what did you think?

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.810
Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:50.169
I've got a video looking at the movie's behind-the-scenes
secrets here, and there'll be a link here

00:05:50.169 --> 00:05:52.879
to my Deadpool Easter Egg video when it's
ready.

00:05:52.879 --> 00:05:57.990
